dows (£211.32). The Zurich policy information (P21, P23) shows a retirement date of 7 June 2031 (age 75), consistent with the report. The Aviva existing plan (P22) confirms access from age 55 (57 from April 2028), with no restriction on the client aged 69. The pension switch report (P15, P17) confirms critical yield at mid growth of 4.8% to age 75, and the new plan being materially more expensive including charges. The fund recommendation (P13) states the investment strategy is based on retirement objectives in isolation as requested, with a diversified multi-asset approach. The intelliflo proposed scenario (P12) states the plan is forward-looking and in real terms. No vulnerability indicators affecting objectives or access needs are evidenced in the retrieved passages. The People's Pension (P19) is retained (not transferred) and is not included in the cashflow model per P10.","discrepancies":["The cashflow model (P10) explicitly lists only three pension inputs (Aviva £3,744, Zurich £103,439, Standard Life £3,002 = £110,185), omitting the retained People's Pension (£6,601.55) and the Scottish Widows encashment (£211.32). The report does not explain this omission, and it is noted as an ambiguity in the extraction metadata.","The report states retirement annual expenditure of approximately £1,700 (P10), while the fact find (P24) records monthly expenditure of £1,718 (approximately £20,616 p.a.). The £1,700 figure in the cashflow appears to be monthly not annual, yet is described as annual in the cashflow narrative — this is internally inconsistent and is not reconciled in the report.","The canonical model records the mid growth rate projection as 2.95% in the critical yield table (P14, P15), while the suitability report narrative (P9) states 'based on a mid-growth rate of 2.94%' — a minor discrepancy of 0.01% already noted in the extraction metadata.","All five recommendations are linked to OBJ2 and/or OBJ3, but the content of OBJ1 is not visible in the canonical extract for this check, and the time horizon array shows [LongTerm, ShortTerm, ShortTerm] — meaning two of three objectives are short-term, yet the sole recommended plan is a pension locked until at least age 57 (client is 69, so this restriction does not apply) with a 6-year accumulation term to age 75.","The report states 'Your state pension income will cover your bills in retirement. Meaning your pension will be allowed to keep growing for large ad hoc spends' (P10), yet the cashflow projects retirement expenditure of approximately £1,700 — if this is monthly (consistent with the fact find figure of £1,718/month and the policy research note of £1,700/month), the state pension of £11,973 p.a. (approximately £998/month) would not cover monthly bills of £1,700, contradicting the report's narrative assertion.","The People's Pension (£6,601.55) is retained but excluded from the cashflow model. No explanation is given in the report as to why a retained asset with material value is excluded from the retirement income projection."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Full text of OBJ1, OBJ2 and OBJ3 as stated in the suitability report (the canonical model provides IDs and time horizons but not the objective descriptions needed to verify whether the recommendation linkages are specific or boilerplate)","Confirmation of whether the £1,700 retirement expenditure figure in the cashflow is monthly or annual (the report is ambiguous and the two readings produce materially different conclusions about adequacy)"],"analysis":"The report's recommendation of a Personal Pension (Aviva Pension Portfolio) with Flexi Access Drawdown is broadly aligned with the client's stated retirement goal: the client is 69, retirement age is set at 75, there is no access restriction concern (minimum pension age does not bite), and FAD is the preferred mechanism. The product wrapper and access mechanism are appropriate for the stated objectives on their face.\n\nHowever, there are three material concerns that prevent a clean No Issue finding.\n\nFirst, the £1,700 retirement expenditure figure is critically ambiguous. The policy research note (P1) and the fact find (P24) both record £1,700 per month as the income need and approximately £1,718 per month as current expenditure respectively. The cashflow narrative (P10) states 'you expect your annual expenditure to be approximately £1,700', which if taken literally would mean £142/month — clearly inconsistent with the fact find. The most likely reading is that £1,700/month was intended, making annual expenditure approximately £20,400. Against this, state pension of £11,973 p.a. (approximately £998/month) would not cover monthly bills, directly contradicting the report's assertion that 'your state pension income will cover your bills in retirement'. If the state pension does not cover bills, the pension fund would need to be drawn down earlier or more heavily than the cashflow model assumes, and the cashflow conclusion of no shortfall to age 100 may be unreliable. This is a material concern going to whether the recommendation can deliver the stated objective.\n\nSecond, the People's Pension (£6,601.55, retained) is excluded from the cashflow model. The report does not explain this omission. While individually modest, this asset forms part of the client's retirement pot and its exclusion means the cashflow projection is incomplete, though directionally conservative.\n\nThird, the objective descriptions (OBJ1, OBJ2, OBJ3) are not available in the evidence pack beyond their IDs and time horizons. The linking of all five recommendations to OBJ2/OBJ3 cannot be confirmed as specific rather than boilerplate without the objective text. The guard against boilerplate linking therefore cannot be cleared.\n\nThe 2.94% vs 2.95% mid growth rate discrepancy is minor and already flagged in the extraction metadata; it does not affect the suitability conclusion materially.\n\nThe vulnerability overlay: no vulnerability characteristics are evidenced in the retrieved passages for this client that would alter the assessment of objectives, access needs or time horizon alignment.\n\nOverall, the £1,700 annual vs monthly ambiguity — and the consequent potential contradiction of the state pension adequacy assertion — is a material concern that the report does not resolve, and the objective descriptions required to verify non-boilerplate linkage are absent.","citations":[{"passageId":"P1","source":"G - Test Case 1 - Policy Research","category":"G","quote":"Needs £1,700 net per month income in retirement.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"I - Suitability Report Test 1","category":"I","quote":"In retirement, you expect your annual expenditure to be approximately £1,700","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"I - Suitability Report Test 1","category":"I","quote":"You receive state pension income of £11,973 per annum","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"I - Suitability Report Test 1","category":"I","quote":"Your state pension income will cover your bills in retirement. Meaning your pension will be allowed to keep growing for large ad hoc spends.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"I - Suitability Report Test 1","category":"I","quote":"I have included the following investments:\no Aviva transfer - £3,744\no Zurich transfer - £103,439\no Standard Life - £3,002","cells":null},{"passageId":"P24","source":"B - Fact Find (Test 1)","category":"B","quote":null,"cells":["Calculated Total Monthly Household Expenditure","£ 1,718.00"]},{"passageId":"P24","source":"B - Fact Find (Test 1)","category":"B","quote":null,"cells":["Total Net Monthly Income","£ 1,430.00","Total Monthly Expenditure","£ 1,718.00","Total Monthly Disposable Income","£ -288.00"]},{"passageId":"P9","source":"I - Suitability Report Test 1","category":"I","quote":"Based on a mid-growth rate of 2.94%, including our advice fees, the recommended plan would need to grow by 4.8% per year up to age 75 in order to meet the 