{"groupId":"G1.10","requirement":"Values expressed in different frequencies or formats reconcile (weekly/monthly/annual, gross/net, percentages and totals)","reportSays":"The suitability report states five income items: (1) Client 1 earns £300 per week net; (2) Client 1 receives £20 per week from Sea Cadets volunteer role; (3) Client 1 receives full State Pension of £230 per week; (4) Kim earns approximately £1,100 per month net; (5) Kim will receive full State Pension of around £230 per week. Expenditure is stated as £1,700 per month. The report also states combined pension value of approximately £110,000 (page 3) and approximately £106,000 (page 4 capacity for loss section), while the Standard Life plan is described as 0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution (page 8) but the charges table shows +0.26% impact for Standard Life (page 11). Growth rate is stated as 2.94% in one place and 2.95% in another.","fileSays":"The fact find (Category B) records: Client 1 basic income as £1,200 net per month (not £1,100); State Pension as £230 per month (not £230 per week); total net monthly income as £1,430; total monthly expenditure as £1,718 (not £1,700). The fact find income table shows two income lines: Basic Income (CDH Recruitment) £1,200 net monthly and State Pension £230 monthly. The five individual pension values sum to £116,998.47 (£6,601.55 + £211.32 + £3,744.36 + £103,439.24 + £3,002.00), matching neither the £110,000 nor the £106,000 figure in the report. The EA5 charges row in the report is computed on Zurich's fund value (£103,438.89 implied) rather than Standard Life's own value of £3,002, making the Standard Life charge comparison unreliable.","discrepancies":["Report states Kim earns approximately £1,100 per month net; fact find records £1,200 net per month (Basic Income, CDH Recruitment) — a difference of £100/month.","Report states Client 1 receives State Pension of £230 per week; fact find records State Pension as £230 per month — a material frequency mismatch (£230/week = ~£996/month vs £230/month).","Report total monthly expenditure stated as £1,700; fact find calculated total monthly household expenditure is £1,718 — a difference of £18/month.","Report states combined pension value of approximately £110,000 (page 3); arithmetic sum of all five individual plan values is £116,998.47 — a difference of approximately £6,998.","Report states pension funds totalling approximately £106,000 (page 4, capacity for loss); arithmetic sum of all five individual plan values is £116,998.47 — a difference of approximately £10,998.","Report uses two different combined pension totals internally: approximately £110,000 (page 3) and approximately £106,000 (page 4) — an internal discrepancy of approximately £4,000.","Report states Standard Life plan is 0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution (page 8 narrative); charges table (page 11) shows +0.26% impact for Standard Life — a contradictory figure for the same comparison.","EA5 charges row is computed using Zurich's fund value (~£103,439) rather than Standard Life's own value of £3,002 — the row is based on the wrong plan value, rendering the Standard Life charge comparison arithmetic unreliable.","Report refers to a mid growth rate of 2.94% in one place and 2.95% in the critical yield table — a minor internal inconsistency.","Fact find total net monthly income is £1,430; this does not reconcile with the income figures the report states (£300/week + £20/week + £230/week + £1,100/month would be approximately £2,378/month if State Pension is weekly as stated in the report, or approximately £1,650/month if State Pension is monthly as in the fact find at £230/month — neither equals £1,430)."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"Several material discrepancies exist between the suitability report and the supporting evidence, and within the report itself.\n\n1. KIM'S INCOME: The report states Kim earns approximately £1,100 per month net, but the fact find (P3) records her net basic monthly income as £1,200. This is a £100/month difference on a named figure — not a rounding issue.\n\n2. STATE PENSION FREQUENCY — CRITICAL: The report states Client 1 receives a State Pension of £230 per week. The fact find (P2) records the State Pension as £230 per month. These use the same cash amount but different frequencies. £230/week annualises to approximately £11,960/year; £230/month annualises to £2,760/year — a difference of over £9,000/year. The full UK State Pension is currently approximately £11,502/year (circa £221/week or £958/month), so the weekly figure is plausible and the monthly figure of £230 is implausibly low. However, this check must rely on the evidence in the file, not external knowledge. The fact find says monthly; the report says weekly — this is a direct frequency mismatch for the same stated amount. The frequency guard (weekly annualises at 52 not 4.33×12) does not explain this away; £230/week and £230/month are simply different figures.\n\n3. EXPENDITURE: The report states expenditure of £1,700/month. The fact find calculated total is £1,718. The difference is £18, which is within the low/rounding category. However the fact find also separately itemises individual expenditure lines summing to £1,718.\n\n4. TOTAL PENSION VALUES — INTERNAL INCONSISTENCY: The report uses £110,000 (page 3) and £106,000 (page 4) as combined pension totals, while the sum of the five individually stated plan values is £116,998.47. None of these three figures agrees with any other. The £110,000 matches the transfer value of only the three plans being switched (£110,185.60), not the total of all five. This is a material internal inconsistency flagged in the extraction.\n\n5. STANDARD LIFE CHARGE: The report narrative (page 8) states the Standard Life plan is 0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution, implying switching saves 0.08%. The charges table (page 11) shows +0.26% impact for Standard Life, implying the new plan is 0.26% more expensive. These are contradictory directions/magnitudes. Additionally, the derived figures confirm EA5's charge row is computed using Zurich's fund value rather than Standard Life's £3,002 value — meaning the row is arithmetically based on the wrong plan. This is a material error in a cost comparison that supports a transfer recommendation.\n\n6. GROWTH RATE: 2.94% vs 2.95% is a minor rounding difference.\n\nThe State Pension frequency mismatch and Kim's income figure both drive the total income picture, which in turn underlies the retirement income needs analysis. The charge figure inconsistency for Standard Life (0.08% vs 0.26%, computed on the wrong fund value) directly affects a cost justification for one of the transfers. These are material concerns that go beyond contextual details.\n\nThe gross/net guard does not apply here as both the report and fact find reference net figures. The rounding guard does not explain the £100 income difference or the frequency mismatch.","citations":[{"passageId":"P2","source":"Fact Find (Test 1).md","category":"B","quote":null,"cells":["","Basic Income","Employed, CDH Recruitment","Monthly","£ 0.00","£ 1,200.00","","","22/09/2025","",""]},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Fact Find (Test 1).md","category":"B","quote":null,"cells":["","State Pension","State Pension","Monthly","£ 0.00","£ 230.00","","","22/09/2025","",""]},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Fact Find (Test 1).md","category":"B","quote":"Calculated Total Monthly Household Expenditure","cells":["Calculated Total Monthly Household Expenditure","£ 1,718.00"]},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Fact Find (Test 1).md","category":"B","quote":null,"cells":["Total Net Monthly Income","£ 1,430.00"]},{"passageId":"P3","source":"Fact Find (Test 1).md","category":"B","quote":null,"cells":["Net Basic Monthly Income","£ 1,200.00",""]},{"passageId":"P12","source":"Test Case 1 - Policy Research -2025.md","category":"G","quote":"Needs £1,700 net per month income in retirement.","cell