ate Pension income as £230.00 per month in the income table, whereas the suitability report (capacity for loss section) states the client is 'in receipt of a full State Pension of approximately £230 per week'. These are materially different figures: £230/month ≈ £2,760/year versus £230/week ≈ £11,960/year.","The fact find records 'Highest rate of income tax paid' as 0%, yet the combined income described in the suitability report (State Pension ~£230/week + HGV earnings ~£300/week net + Sea Cadets £20/week ≈ £550/week gross equivalent) would typically place the client above the personal allowance threshold, making a 0% tax rate difficult to reconcile unless net figures are used throughout. This is a potential inconsistency in the recorded tax status.","The incomeTaxBand, annualAllowance (as applied to this client), and territorialProfile are absent from the canonical model, meaning the report's implicit tax treatment (basic rate relief on contributions, marginal rate on drawdown withdrawals) cannot be fully verified against a recorded tax position.","No supporting document (HMRC correspondence, provider confirmation, or fact find field) directly evidences that the MPAA has not been triggered, beyond the report's own assertion."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Client's recorded income tax band (absent from canonical model and not explicitly stated in the fact find beyond the 0% field)","Territorial/residency profile (absent from canonical model — required to confirm UK tax treatment applies)","Direct evidence of MPAA non-trigger status (no HMRC or provider document confirms this)"],"analysis":"The most material discrepancy is the State Pension frequency mismatch: the fact find income table records £230/month whereas the suitability report states £230/week. These differ by a factor of approximately 4.3 and the weekly figure of £230 (≈£11,960/year) is broadly consistent with the full new State Pension rate for 2024/2025 (approximately £221.20/week), making