ribed as arriving at an agreed risk level through discussion. No additional notes were recorded under 'Investment target' or 'Agreed risk level'. The suitability report itself (P9, P10, P14) confirms the recommendation narrative including objectives and charges. The charges table (internal inconsistency noted in extraction) shows the Standard Life impact as +0.26%, contradicting the report's stated 0.08% saving. The EA5 charge rows are computed on Zurich's fund value (£103,438) rather than Standard Life's (£3,002), making the Standard Life cost comparison unreliable. No retirement income projection is present. C2's assets, liabilities, ATR and capacity for loss are absent from the report.","discrepancies":["The report states the Standard Life plan is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution' (a saving), but the charges comparison table shows the impact of the recommended alternative for Standard Life as +0.26% (an increase in cost to the client). These are contradictory.","The EA5 charge rows are computed using Zurich's fund value (~£103,439) rather than Standard Life's own value (£3,002), meaning the entire cost comparison for Standard Life is based on the wrong plan value and is arithmetically unreliable.","The report's rationale bullets for Aviva switch, Zurich switch and Standard Life switch include several sentences that are verbatim identical across all three recommendations ('The new scheme offers access to a wider range of investment options...', 'The new scheme allows adviser charges to be deducted directly...', 'The adviser will have the ability to actively monitor and adjust investments as required'), which meet the definition of boilerplate under the comparison guard.","The ATR questionnaire produced a score of 4, but the report records an agreed rating of 5. The risk profile document records no additional notes under 'Agreed risk level', providing no documented basis for the override.","No retirement income projection is present in the report