llustration [P20] shows fund charges of 1.0% less discount of 0.5% = net 0.50%. The 0.18% recorded in the report for EA5 appears to correspond to Zurich's fund charge, not Standard Life's — consistent with the derived figures flag that EA5 rows are computed on Zurich's fund value (£103,439) rather than Standard Life's (£3,002).","3. Standard Life cost comparison contradiction (already noted in internal inconsistencies): report narrative (page 8) states Standard Life existing plan is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution', implying a saving from switching; the charges table (page 11) shows impact of recommended alternative for Standard Life as '+0.26%', implying switching is more expensive. These are directly contradictory.","4. Transaction costs: the SIPP research report [P10] discloses fund-level transaction costs of £145.05 (0.26%) for CT Universal MAP Balanced C Acc as a separate quantified line. The suitability report excludes transaction costs with a stated reason. The research report quantifies them, but the suitability report does not include even an estimated figure despite a quantified figure being available in the file.","5. EA5 charge comparison rows in the charges table appear to be computed on Zurich's fund value (£103,439) rather than Standard Life's fund value (£3,002), per derived figures: EA5 existing annual charge of £186.19 at 0.18% and EA5 impact of £268.94 at 0.26% both imply a fund value of approximately £103,439, not £3,002. This renders the Standard Life cost comparison unreliable.","6. Zurich existing plan: report records EA4 existing charge as 0.93%. Zurich policy info [P7] discloses a bid-to-offer spread of 5% on all payments and AMC of 0.75% pa plus additional charges. Whether 0.93% correctly captures all Zurich charges (including the bid-to-offer spread impact) is not fully reconcilable from the illustration alone, though 0.93% as an ongoing annual charge figure is plausible if the bid-to-offer spread is treated as a one-of