going fee is corroborated in P5. The report states the ongoing fee is 'minus the initial fee' but 1% of the full £110,185 = £1,101.85, not 1% of £110,185 less £3,305.55 — however the report uses 'circa' and references 'expected contributions', and the guard notes the monetary fee moves with transfer value, so this is a low-level rounding/basis ambiguity rather than a material contradiction. The key limitation is that the client agreement document in the file sets fees by reference to the suitability report (circular), and no bespoke signed fee schedule exists in the pack as an independent corroborating document. The matchesClientAgreement field being null means the report does not itself make an explicit consistency assertion. The standard terms are consistent with the rates charged. No vulnerability factors in the file are identified as materially affecting this cost/charges check. Overall, the rates are consistent with the standard client agreement on file, the monetary amounts are arithmetically supportable, and the services attached to the ongoing fee are described. The absence of a bespoke signed fee schedule is a modest gap but the standard terms document corroborates the rates. No material contradiction is present.","citations":[{"passageId":"P9","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"For researching, recommending and arranging these investments, we charge an initial advice fee of 3% of the amount invested as per the terms stated in our Client Agreement. In monetary terms this is £3,305.55 with current transfer values, this figure may fluctuate depending on transfer values. This will be paid directly by the pension schemes on transfer.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P5","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"The costs for this service are 1% per annum. Based on current values & expected contributions, minus the initial fee, this would be equal to circa £1,101.85 for the first year.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P