 (£3,002), making the EA5 row arithmetically unreliable. (2) The SIPP research report [P10] shows MiFID II pre-sale cost disclosures for the two recommended funds: HSBC Global Strategy Balanced Portfolio C Acc — ongoing 0.18%, transaction 0.01%, total 0.19%; CT Universal MAP Balanced C — ongoing 0.29%, transaction 0.26%, total 0.55%; aggregate total £406.74 (0.37%). Transaction costs are present in the research report but the suitability report's transaction costs monetary field is absent (extraction flag). (3) The Aviva Key Features [P4, P8] confirm flexi-access drawdown, UFPLS, annuity and phased drawdown options are available on the new plan. (4) The existing arrangements table [P19] shows Scottish Widows offers only 'Full and partial UFPLS' and People's Pension offers 'FAD, Full and partial UFPLS' — meaning FAD is already available on People's Pension (retained) and was available on Aviva (transferred). Zurich offers only UFPLS (no FAD), Standard Life offers only FAD. (5) Policy research [P5, P7] confirms Zurich has no FAD, Standard Life has FAD but not UFPLS; the new Aviva plan has both. (6) The Zurich policy info [P9] confirms no guaranteed annuity rates are flagged. (7) The SIPP research report [P3, P12] confirms the Aviva Pension Portfolio — Choice was the recommended product selected from 118 products. (8) The mid-growth rate is described as 2.95% in the critical yield table (page 18) but 2.94% in the page 11 narrative — a minor inconsistency in the report itself. (9) The like-for-like saving of 0.24%/£264 versus 4.24%/£4,672 more expensive including adviser fees is stated in the report; the £4,672 at 4.24% implies a fund value of £110,188.68 which is broadly consistent with the £110,185 transfer value. (10) No switching analysis or separate cost comparison document is present in the retrieved passages corroborating the charges table figures independently.","discrepancies":["The report states (page 8) the Standard Life plan is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution' (implying a saving by switching). The charges comparison table (page 11, per the canonical internal inconsistencies) shows the impact of the recommended alternative for Standard Life as +0.26% — meaning the new plan is MORE expensive for Standard Life, directly contradicting the page 8 saving claim.","The derived figures confirm EA5's existing annual charge (£186.19 at 0.18%) and EA5 impact (£268.94 at 0.26%) are both computed on Zurich's fund value (~£103,439) rather than Standard Life's actual fund value (£3,002), rendering the Standard Life cost comparison row arithmetically unreliable and unsupported.","The 'Lower Costs' benefit is stated as a general benefit for the Aviva recommendation, yet on the Standard Life transfer the report's own table shows cost increases (+0.26%), undermining the lower-costs justification for that specific transfer. The report does not separately address this.","The report states a mid-growth rate of 2.94% (page 11) but the critical yield table (page 18) labels it as 2.95% — a minor internal inconsistency in the justification figures used.","Transaction costs are disclosed in the SIPP research report [P10] (CT Universal MAP: 0.26% transaction costs, aggregate transaction costs £147.80/0.13%) but the suitability report's transaction costs monetary figure is absent per the extraction flag, meaning the all-in cost disclosed to the client may not include transaction costs.","The 'Enhanced Retirement Flexibility' benefit claims the new scheme provides 'more flexible retirement options'. The existing Aviva plan (being transferred) already offered FAD and UFPLS per the existing arrangements table, so this benefit does not represent an improvement over that specific ceding plan. However, Zurich offered only UFPLS (no FAD) and Standard Life offered only FAD (no UFPLS), so flexibility is genuinely improved for those two transfers.","The 'Lower Costs' benefit as a general statement is contradicted for Standard Life specifically, and the report makes no distinction between plans where costs are lower and plans where costs are higher when presenting the benefits section."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The central concern in this check is whether the benefits stated to justify a higher cost are supported by and consistent with the evidence. The report acknowledges the plan is 4.24% more expensive including adviser fees and provides a critical yield justification. Several of the benefits have evidential support: Greater Investment Choice is supported by the Aviva Key Features and SIPP research confirming access to 6,900+ funds; Adviser Charging Facilitation is a mechanical feature confirmed by the product literature; Ongoing Monitoring is a service proposition benefit; Enhanced Retirement Flexibility is partially supported — Zurich had no FAD and Standard Life had no UFPLS, so the new plan does improve flexibility for those transfers, though the guard is engaged for the Aviva-to-Aviva transfer where FAD was already available. The most material issue is the 'Lower Costs' benefit and the Standard Life cost comparison. The report states on page 8 that the Standard Life plan is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution', asserting a cost saving. The charges table on page 11 shows +0.26% — meaning the new plan is more expensive for Standard Life. This is a direct contradiction between the report's own narrative and its own table. The derived figures make this worse: the EA5 charge row is calculated on Zurich's fund value (~£103,439) not Standard Life's (£3,002), so the monetary figures in that row are arithmetically wrong by roughly a factor of 34. This means the cost comparison for Standard Life is doubly flawed — the direction is wrong in the narrative, and the monetary calculation is performed on the wrong fund. This is a material discrepancy: the 'Lower Costs' benefit cannot be supported for the Standard Life transfer, and no separate justification for a cost increase on that transfer is provided. The transaction costs omission from the suitability report is a moderate concern: the research report shows CT Universal MAP has transaction costs of 0.26% — a non-trivial amount — which appear not to be included in the all-in cost figure presented to the client. The minor 2.94%/2.95% growth rate inconsistency is low severity. The vulnerability overlay: the extraction flags the assessmentOutcome for both clients as absent. No specific vulnerability characteristics are identified in the retrieved passages that would materially alter this assessment, so no overlay adjustment is made. On balance, the Standard Life cost contradiction (direction of saving vs. actual increase, compounded by the wrong fund value being used in the calculation) is a genuine material discrepancy that is not explained by any false-positive guard. The 'Lower Costs' benefit stated as a general justification for the recommendation is demonstrably incorrect for at least one of the three transfers, and the report provides no remediation of this. Severity is High because the report claims a cost saving that its own comparison table contradicts, per the severity guidance.","citations":[{"passageId":"P19","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Standard Life - D2301334000","FAD","300","Yes",""]},{"passageId":"P21","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"By switching to this scheme, you will benefit from lower costs, greater investment flexibility, and continued professional oversight, ensuring your pension remains on track to support your long-term financial goals.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P21","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Greater Investment Choice - The new scheme offers access to a wider range of investment options, allowing for a more tailored investment strategy that better suits your risk profile and retirement goals, and allowing greater choice should your risk profile change in the future.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"SIPP research report.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["CT Universal MAP Balanced Fund C Acc","-","£159.77  (0.29%)","£145.05  (0.26%)","-","£304.82  (0.55%)"]},{"passageId":"P10","source":"SIPP research report.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Total aggregated fund costs:","£0.00  (0.00%)","£258.93  (0.24%)","£147.80  (0.13%)","£0.00  (0.00%)","£406.74  (0.37%)"]},{"passageId":"P22","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["HSBC Global Strategy Balanced Portfolio C Acc","50%","5","0.18%"]},{"passageId":"P22","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["CT Universal MAP Balanced C","50%","5","0.29%"]},{"passageId":"P20","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Aviva Pension Portfolio","£110,185","£0"]},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Key Features (1).md","category":"H","quote":"take up to 25% of your pension pot tax free as a lump sum and/or as a regular income, and then take the remaining pension pot as and when you need it through income drawdown whilst continuing to manage the investments within your pension pot.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P8","source":"Key Features (1).md","category":"H","quote":"You can choose to draw down any level of income your pension pot can support.","cells":null}],"severity":"High","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

