ation) confirms platform charge 0.21%; weighted average investment charge 0.23% (not 0.22% as the report states). [P3] also confirms the new plan total 0.44% / £484.81 and the £4,672 / 4.24% including adviser fees. The derived figures flag that EA5's monetary amounts (£186.19 at 0.18% implies fund value ~£103,439, which is Zurich's value, not Standard Life's £3,002); EA5 impact (£268.94 at 0.26% also implies ~£103,439). [P19] states mid-growth rate of 2.94%; [P21] labels the mid-growth rate as 2.95% in the critical yield table. No provider documentation confirms 0.18% as Standard Life's all-in charge — the Standard Life illustration [P8] shows 0.5% net. The pension switch research report [P6] lists an investment charge of 0.18% for one fund but does not name Standard Life. The research report [P18] notes all Standard Life funds in the same range are more expensive than Aviva.","discrepancies":["1. EA5 (Standard Life) monetary charge £186.19 at 0.18% implies a fund value of ~£103,439 (Zurich's value), not Standard Life's own value of £3,002. The charge and impact rows for Standard Life appear to have been calculated on the wrong fund value (Zurich's), making the cost comparison for Standard Life arithmetically incorrect and unreliable.","2. EA5 impact amount £268.94 at 0.26% likewise implies ~£103,439 (Zurich's value), confirming the Standard Life row is computed on Zurich's plan value rather than Standard Life's £3,002.","3. The report (page 8) states Standard Life is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution' (i.e. switching saves 0.08%), but the charges table on page 11 shows the impact for Standard Life as +0.26% — a cost increase, not a saving. These are directly contradictory.","4. Standard Life's existing annual charge is stated as 0.18% in the comparison table, but the Standard Life policy illustration [P8] shows fund charge 1.0% less discount 0.5% = net 0.5%, not 0.18%. No provider document in the pack confirms 0.18% as the Standard Life all-in charge.","5. The Aviva illustration [P35] states the weighted average investment charge as 0.23%, whereas the suitability report states 0.22% (investment management charge). This is a minor discrepancy between the illustration and the report.","6. Mid-growth rate is stated as 2.94% on page 11 narrative [P19] but as 2.95% in the critical yield table on page 18 [P21] — an internal inconsistency within the report.","7. Transaction costs for underlying funds are excluded from all charge figures; the report acknowledges this [P3] but states they are accounted for in net performance figures. No monetary estimate is provided.","8. The Zurich policy documentation [P2] discloses a bid-offer spread of 5% and AMC of 0.75%; these are not separately reconciled against the 0.93% total used in the comparison table, though the 0.93% could reflect a combined or blended rate. No provider document in the pack explicitly confirms 0.93% as the current all-in annual recurring charge."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The most significant finding is the Standard Life (EA5) row in the charges comparison table. The derived figures establish conclusively that the monetary charge (£186.19) and impact (£268.94) attributed to Standard Life were both calculated using a fund value of approximately £103,439 — which is Zurich's fund value — rather than Standard Life's own current value of £3,002. This is not a rounding difference; it is a factor of ~34 error in the base. As a result, the percentage figures in that row (0.18% existing, +0.26% impact) cannot be accepted as reliable representations of the cost differential for Standard Life. This arithmetic error directly underlies the separate internal contradiction: page 8 of the report claims Standard Life is 0.08% more expensive (a saving for switching), while page 11's table shows +0.26% (a cost increase from switching). Both cannot be correct, and the derived figures confirm the table row is itself wrong. The Standard Life policy illustration [P8] further shows a net charge of 0.5%, not 0.18%, adding a third inconsistent figure. The overall like-for-like saving of 0.24% / £264 is therefore computed from a table that contains at least one materially wrong row (Standard Life), making the aggregate comparison unreliable. The Aviva illustration [P35] shows a weighted average IMC of 0.23% vs the report's stated 0.22% — a low-severity discrepancy but present. The mid-growth rate discrepancy (2.94% vs 2.95%) is a rounding/labelling issue of low materiality. The bid-offer spread on the Zurich plan (5%) is not reflected in the ongoing charge comparison, though the 0.93% total may absorb most ongoing costs; the pack does not contain a document explicitly confirming 0.93% as the current all-in Zurich recurring charge, though the figure is self-consistent with the plan value. The Comparisons on different bases guard is noted: the 0.24% cheaper and 4.24% more expensive figures use different bases (excluding and including adviser fees respectively) and are not contradictory in themselves, but the Standard Life row error contaminates the 0.24% figure. Transaction costs are excluded with an explanation [P3], which partially mitigates that omission, though no estimate is given. No vulnerability indicators are present in the pack that would alter this analysis. The Standard Life arithmetic error and the directly contradictory cost-saving claim are genuine findings that survive all guards.","citations":[{"passageId":"P3","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Standard Life","0.18%","£186.19","+0.26%","+£268.94"]},{"passageId":"P3","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"the Aviva plan is 0.24% less expensive than your current plans","cells":null},{"passageId":"P3","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"your proposed plan can underperform your current plan by £264 in the first year (in monetary terms)","cells":null},{"passageId":"P3","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"The charges quoted in this report exclude the transaction costs of the underlying funds, as these are variable and subject to change based on factors such as market conditions and trading activity.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P8","source":"Standard Life Policy illustration.md","category":"E","quote":null,"cells":["Fund charges","1.0%","Discounts","-0.5%","Total charge","0.5%"]},{"passageId":"P19","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Based on a mid-growth rate of 2.94%, including our advice fees, the recommended plan would need to grow by 4.8% per year up to age 75 in order to meet the same value as your current plan.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P21","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Mid growth rate - (2.95%)","Projected value at age 75","£127,000","£115,000","Reduction in yield [1]","0.3%","2.2%","Critical yield [2]","-","4.8%","Effect of switching [3]","-","£12,000 or 9.4% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P35","source":"Illustration - new.md","category":"H","quote":null,"cells":["CT Universal MAP Balanced C Acc GB00BF99W060","0.29%","HSBC Global Strategy Balanced Portfolio C Acc GB00B76WP695","0.18%","Weighted Average charge","0.23%"]},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Standard Life - D2301334000","£3,002","£3,002","£0","This pension arrangement does not contain any safeguarded benefits","0.18% fund and 0.34% platform"]}],"severity":"High","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

