s an immediate death-benefit shortfall of £3,305 (existing £110,185.60 vs new solution day-one £106,880.60). The switch report contains per-plan critical yield analyses for Zurich-only and Aviva+Zurich+Standard Life combined, showing materially different loss scenarios from those in the suitability report's appendix (e.g. Zurich-only mid-growth effect: £3,000 / 2.4% decrease vs the combined figure of £12,000 / 9.4% used in the report). The charges comparison table in the suitability report shows Standard Life EA5 impact as +0.26%, contradicting the page 8 narrative of a 0.08% saving. Derived figures confirm the EA5 charges row is computed on Zurich's fund value (£103,439) rather than Standard Life's (£3,002), rendering the Standard Life charge comparison unreliable. The switch report shows no information about features or guarantees lost on any existing arrangement. The suitability report extraction confirms featuresOrGuaranteesLost is NotPresentInDocument for all five existing arrangements (EA1–EA5). No tax analysis beyond the Scottish Widows small-pots discussion is evidenced in the file.","discrepancies":["The charges table on page 11 shows Standard Life (EA5) impact as +0.26% (an increase in cost), but the page 8 recommendation narrative states the Standard Life plan is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution' (i.e. a cost saving of 0.08%). These are contradictory.","Derived figures confirm the EA5 charges row is computed using Zurich's fund value (~£103,439) rather than Standard Life's own value (~£3,002), so the monetary and percentage impact figures for Standard Life in the charges table are unreliable and misleading.","The suitability report's critical yield appendix presents a single combined table for all three switching pensions; the switch report (G) contains separate per-plan tables. The combined presentation may obscure the relatively small loss scenario for the individual Standard Life and Aviva plans.","No features, guarantees or protections being given up are described for any of the five existing arrangements (confirmed NotPresentInDocument by extraction for EA1–EA5). The report does not address what, if any, safeguarded benefits or contractual protections attach to any plan being switched.","No tax consequences of the pension consolidation (beyond the Scottish Widows small-pots encashment discussion) are addressed in the report, notwithstanding that the initial adviser charge of £3,305 is deducted from the pension fund, reducing the tax-free cash entitlement at retirement.","The report presents a like-for-like charge saving (0.24% / £264) and an all-in cost increase (4.24% / £4,672) without clearly reconciling them for the client; the juxtaposition could mislead a client reading only the like-for-like figure.","The mid-growth rate used in the critical yield table is labelled '2.95%' on page 18 but the page 11 narrative refers to '2.94%' — a minor internal inconsistency.","The loss scenarios in the critical yield appendix are presented only in the appendix; the body of the report (page 8 'Other Considerations of Switching') does not quantify any monetary loss scenario — it refers the reader to the appendix for critical yield detail."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The report does have a dedicated risk section ('Other Considerations of Switching', page 8) in the main body and is not confined to the appendix, which satisfies the basic prominence test. Standard investment risk warnings, the no-guarantee statement and the death-benefit shortfall (£3,305) are stated. The critical yield loss scenarios are quantified and consistent with the supporting switch report evidence at the combined level.\n\nHowever, several material gaps remain:\n\n1. STANDARD LIFE CHARGE DISCREPANCY: The page 8 narrative claims Standard Life is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution' (a saving). The charges table on page 11 shows +0.26% (an increase). Derived figures confirm the EA5 charges row uses Zurich's fund value (~£103,439) rather than Standard Life's (~£3,002) — meaning the charges table figure itself is computed on the wrong plan. This is a material error: the client is told switching Standard Life saves money (page 8 narrative) when the table shows the opposite, and neither figure is reliable. This is not explained in the guard for false positives; it is a genuine internal contradiction in the report.\n\n2. FEATURES/GUARANTEES LOST: No features or guarantees lost are described for any existing arrangement. The extraction confirms this is absent from the document (NotPresentInDocument for EA1–EA5). Where existing pensions may carry any contractual protections, the absence of any disclosure is a material omission under COBS 9.4.7R.\n\n3. TAX CONSEQUENCES: The initial adviser charge of £3,305 is deducted from the pension fund on day one, reducing the capital available to generate tax-free cash at retirement. This tax consequence is not addressed in the report. The Scottish Widows encashment is discussed separately but the broader tax impact of the consolidation is not.\n\n4. LOSS SCENARIO PROMINENCE: The monetary loss scenarios (£10,400–£15,000) appear only in the appendix (page 18). The page 8 risk section refers to the critical yield appendix but does not itself quantify any monetary loss. Benefits (lower costs, greater investment choice, flexibility) are stated prominently in the body of the report, while the quantified downside appears only in the appendix — this is a prominence concern, albeit moderated by the fact that the referral to the appendix is explicit.\n\n5. CHARGE PRESENTATION: The simultaneous presentation of a 0.24%/£264 like-for-like saving and a 4.24%/£4,672 all-in cost increase without clear reconciliation may mislead. The guards note this is not strictly contradictory but the report's failure to clearly explain the relationship is a fair presentation concern.\n\nThe vulnerability overlay: the file records John as a risk-4 questionnaire result overridden to risk-5, with the client confirming awareness. No specific vulnerability characteristics are recorded that would heighten the concern beyond what is already identified above, but the complexity of the charge presentation and the absence of clear features-lost disclosure would apply equally to any client.\n\nOverall: the most material concern is the Standard Life charge contradiction (page 8 narrative vs page 11 table, compounded by the wrong fund value used in the table) and the absence of any features/guarantees-lost disclosure. These are High-severity gaps. The tax consequence of the initial charge reducing future tax-free cash is also unaddressed. Severity: High.","citations":[{"passageId":"P6","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"The plan itself is 0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution","cells":null},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Standard Life","0.18%","£186.19","+0.26%","£268.94"]},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"You should note that whilst the transactions are pending, there could be movements within the stock-market; that is to say that the stock-market","cells":null},{"passageId":"P5","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"A switch between pension providers does not guarantee a greater pension fund at retirement and there is no guarantee that the new provider's funds will outperform those of your existing provider.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Effect of switching","","-","£10,400 or 9.6% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Effect of switching [3]","","-","£12,000 or 9.4% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Effect of switchi