vy, or the risk that the recommended funds are rated risk level 3–4 by the fund managers themselves (HSBC GSB Portfolio rated 3; CT Universal MAP Balanced Fund is rated 4 per KIID), while the client's agreed ATR is 5.","fileSays":"The product literature on file (Aviva Key Features, KIIDs for HSBC Global Strategy Balanced Portfolio and CT Universal MAP Balanced Fund, Fund Factsheet, Terms and Conditions, Zurich Policy Info) discloses the following material risks not fully replicated in the report: (a) HSBC KIID: counterparty risk, credit risk, default risk, derivatives risk, currency risk, liquidity risk at fund level, emerging markets risk (P3, P11); fund rated 3 on risk/reward scale (P1, P5); investment not appropriate for investors planning to withdraw within 5 years (P3, P9); (b) CT Universal MAP Balanced KIID: derivative risk (losses can be greater than initial investment), fixed interest securities risk, credit risk, smaller companies risk, emerging markets risk, liquidity risk, currency risk, leverage risk (P11); fund rated 4 on risk/reward scale (P9); (c) Aviva Key Features: income drawdown risks — impact on means-tested benefits, fund depletion from high income withdrawals, less money for annuity/dependants, tax position affecting income drawdown payments (P4); pension pot value and retirement benefits may go down as well as up, could get back less than invested (P14); property fund dealing delays (P4); (d) Zurich Policy Info: property fund dealing delays up to 12 months, derivatives risk, OEIC cross-liability risk, inflation erosion of fixed-interest income (P17); transfer checklist noting features or benefits that may have been forgotten (P16); (e) Terms and Conditions: dilution levy, SDRT, ETI trading charges (P10, P13); (f) Risk Profile Report: natural ATR questionnaire score was 4 (Cautious Balanced), overridden to agreed level 5 (P18); HSBC fund is risk level 3, CT fund is risk level 4 — both below the agreed ATR of 5, a mismatch not discussed in the report as a product-specific risk.","discrepancies":["The report discloses a general capital-at-risk/market risk warning but does not disclose counterparty risk, credit/default risk, derivatives risk, currency/FX risk, emerging markets risk, leverage risk, or liquidity risk at fund level — all of which appear in the KIIDs for the two recommended funds (HSBC GSB Portfolio and CT Universal MAP Balanced Fund).","The report does not disclose that HSBC Global Strategy Balanced Portfolio is rated risk level 3 (out of 5) and CT Universal MAP Balanced Fund is rated risk level 4, while the client's agreed ATR is 5; no product-specific discussion of this fund-level risk rating mismatch appears in the report.","The report does not address income drawdown-specific risks identified in the Aviva Key Features: impact on means-tested benefits, fund depletion from high income withdrawals, reduced provision for dependants or future annuity purchase, or tax-position-related variation in income drawdown payments.","The report does not mention the 5-year minimum holding period recommendation stated in the KIIDs (HSBC and CT funds both state the fund may not be appropriate for investors planning to withdraw within 5 years).","The report does not disclose the Zurich property fund dealing delay risk (up to 12 months) or OEIC cross-liability risk from the existing Zurich policy literature.","The report does not mention dilution levy, SDRT or ETI trading charges disclosed in the Aviva Terms and Conditions.","The performance comparison risk (report states recommended funds have not outperformed existing Zurich funds) is disclosed, but no quantified or illustrated loss scenario flowing from this underperformance relative to the existing Zurich holding is provided in the context of why switching is still recommended.","The report's lifestyle risk drift warning relates to the existing funds being switched away from, not to the recommended funds — no equivalent forward-looking risk about the recommended fund's own risk profile trajectory is given.","No vulnerability-specific communication adjustment or additional risk explanation is recorded in the report, and no formal vulnerability assessment outcome is present in the canonical model (both /vulnerability/C1/assessmentOutcome and /vulnerability/C2/assessmentOutcome are absent), meaning any heightened need for prominent or simplified risk disclosure cannot be confirmed as addressed."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The report contains a general capital-at-risk warning, a lifestyle drift risk, a performance comparison note, cashflow projection caveats, IHT legislative risk, cancellation risks and a liquidity access delay. These are meaningful disclosures. However, the KIIDs for both recommended funds (HSBC Global Strategy Balanced Portfolio and CT Universal MAP Balanced Fund) disclose a material set of product-specific and investment-specific risks — counterparty risk, credit and default risk, derivatives risk, currency risk, emerging markets risk, liquidity risk at fund level, leverage risk — none of which appear in the suitability report. The guard confirms that a general capital-at-risk warning does not satisfy the requirement to disclose product-specific risks; these must be tested separately. The fund-level risk ratings (HSBC rated 3, CT rated 4) sit below the client's agreed ATR of 5, and the report nowhere discusses this as a product-specific risk or limitation. The Aviva Key Features document identifies income drawdown-specific risks (means-tested benefits, fund depletion, annuity provision) that are absent from the report. The KIIDs also flag that neither fund is appropriate for investors intending to withdraw within 5 years — a material product-specific limitation that is not mentioned in the report. Taken together, the omission of these KIID-identified risks means the report has not fairly and prominently explained all material product-specific and investment-specific risks. The guard against deferring risk disclosure to product literature is engaged: there is no statement in the report that all other risks are in the literature provided, so the risks are simply absent rather than delegated, which is the more serious position. On vulnerability: the canonical model records no formal vulnerability assessment outcome for either client, so it cannot be confirmed that any heightened disclosure needs have been addressed. This adds a further layer of concern but is primarily assessed under CHK-010. Severity: the omission of KIID-identified product-specific risks (counterparty, derivatives, currency, credit, leverage, emerging markets) from a recommendation involving two OEIC funds is a material gap — assessed as Moderate to High. The income drawdown risk omission and the fund risk-rating mismatch with agreed ATR push this toward High.","citations":[{"passageId":"P3","source":"KIIDs (1).md","category":"H","quote":null,"cells":["Counterparty Risk","Credit Risk","Default Risk","Derivatives Risk","asset allocation: Equities 45-70%, Bonds 20-55%, Other 0-25%"]},{"passageId":"P11","source":"KIIDs (1).md","category":"H","quote":"Derivative Risk: derivative values rise and fall at a greater rate than equities and debt instruments. Losses can be greater than the initial investment.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P11","source":"KIIDs (1).md","category":"H","quote":"Currency Risk: your investment may be adversely affected by changes in currency exchange rates.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P11","source":"KIIDs (1).md","category":"H","quote":"Emerging Markets Risk: this Fund invests in emerging markets which are less developed and have additional legal and political risks.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P11","source":"KIIDs (1).md","category":"H","quote":"Liquidity Risk: the ability to buy and sell assets at a favourable price may be affected by a low level of counterparties willing to enter into a transaction with the Fund.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P11","source":"KIIDs (1).md","category":"H","quote":"Leverage Risk: the use of derivatives to increase the exposure of the Fund to the market through either long or short positions will make the value of the Fund's investments more volatile than those of an unleveraged fund.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P9","source":"KIIDs (1).md","category":"H","quote":"Recommendation: this fund may not be appropriate for investors who plan to withdraw their money within 5 years.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P9","source":"KIIDs (1).md","category":"H","quote":"The Fund is rated 4 because historically it has shown a medium level of volatility (how much the value of the Fund goes up and down).","cells":null},{"passageId":"P1","source":"KIIDs (1).md","category":"H","quote":"This is one of a range of actively managed Global Strategy Portfolios offered at five different risk levels. The Fund is rated as 3, where 1 is a lower level of risk and 5 is a higher level of risk.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Key Features (1).md","category":"H","quote":"The income you take may have an impact on any means-tested benefits you're receiving. You may want to discuss this with your financial adviser.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Key Features (1).md","category":"H","quote":"Your investments need to grow to compensate for the income you withdraw. If that doesn't happen, the income you take will deplete your pension fund. Especially if you choose to take a high level of income.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Key Features (1).md","category":"H","quote":"If you withdraw high levels of income, there'll be less money to provide for dependants or to buy a lifetime annuity in the future, should you want to.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P14","source":"Key Features (1).md","category":"H","quote":"The value of your pension pot and the retirement benefits it can provide may go down as well as up.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P17","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","