{"groupId":"G8.8","requirement":"Material risks or limitations identified in the evidence but not disclosed in the report","reportSays":"The suitability report discloses the following material disadvantages and risks: (1) Including adviser fees, the Aviva plan is 4.24% more expensive than current plans, requiring £4,672 outperformance in year one. (2) At mid growth rate of 2.94%, the new plan must grow at 4.8% per year to age 75 to match the existing plan. (3) Loss scenarios at three growth rates: low = £10,400/9.6% decrease; mid = £12,000/9.4% decrease; high = £15,000/10.0% decrease. (4) Retaining People's Pension because transferring would lose employer contributions. (5) Recommended Aviva funds have not outperformed current Zurich funds, which are in higher-risk funds more susceptible to market volatility. (6) Limitations: transaction costs excluded from charge quotes; charge monetary figures may differ from illustration; Standard Life performance data unavailable; cashflow assumes surplus income is spent. (7) Product-specific risks: 10-15 working day liquidity delay; cancellation notice — previous provider not obliged to take plan back; investment fall during cancellation deducted; IHT legislation change from 6 April 2027 on unused DC pensions. The report does not disclose any features or guarantees lost on transfer (featuresOrGuaranteesLost paths all marked NotPresentInDocument).","fileSays":"The pension switch report (Category G) corroborates the three-growth-rate switching effect figures (£10,400/9.6%, £12,000/9.4%, £15,000/10.0%) and critical yield of 4.8% at mid rate. It also shows a mid growth rate of 2.95% (not 2.94% as stated in the report's narrative). The Standard Life policy info (Category E) discloses that the Standard Life plan benefits from a Differential Fund Discount / Leaver Discount of 0.48% which 'may not be available from other pension providers upon transfer' — a plan-specific benefit the client would lose on transfer. The Zurich policy info (Category E) discloses potential exit charges on capital units reducing by ~0.3%–0.4% per month until SRA, and that Zurich does not currently offer partial UFPLS or phased PCLS without drawdown — features that may not be replicated. The Zurich transfer checklist (Category E) prompts consideration of life cover, waiver benefits, guaranteed annuity rates, and fund choices. The Aviva policy info (Category E) notes the existing plan does not allow adviser charging and that any changes to the plan (such as transferring out) won't affect tax-free lump sum entitlement on any other Aviva plan. The pension switch report shows no benefits listed as lost for any existing product. The SIPP research report and KIIDs (Category H) contain generic market risk, counterparty, credit, default and derivatives risks for the recommended funds. The Aviva Key Features (Category H) discloses income drawdown risks including means-tested benefits impact, fund depletion from high income withdrawals, and tax risks.","discrepancies":["The Standard Life policy info discloses a Differential Fund Discount / Leaver Discount of 0.4800% that 'may not be available from other pension providers upon transfer' — this is a material benefit the client loses on switching Standard Life, but the report makes no mention of this discount or its loss anywhere in the risk/disadvantage disclosures.","The report states mid growth rate as 2.94% in the narrative; the pension switch report (P10, P2, P3) uses 2.95% — a minor but present numeric discrepancy.","The Zurich policy info discloses a potential exit charge on capital units if the fund is transferred, capped at 1% from age 55, reducing by ~0.3–0.4% per month until SRA. The report does not explicitly disclose this potential Zurich exit charge as a risk/disadvantage to the client.","The Aviva Key Features document (P11) discloses income drawdown risks (means-tested benefits, fund depletion, tax risks) which are not referenced in the report — though this may be because the recommendation is accumulation rather than drawdown withdrawal at this stage.","The pension switch report records 'No benefits were listed for the existing products' (P3), yet the Standard Life policy info identifies the Leaver Discount as a benefit specific to the plan. This creates a gap between the switch report and the underlying policy information.","The EA5 (Standard Life) charge computation uses Zurich's fund value (£103,438.89) rather than Standard Life's own value (£3,002), making the Standard Life charges comparison unreliable — a material limitation not disclosed to the client in the report.","The internal inconsistency noted in the extraction — Standard Life recommendation on page 8 states '0.08% more expensive' while the charges table on page 11 shows '+0.26%' — means the report gives the client contradictory information about whether switching Standard Life saves or costs money, which affects the client's ability to assess the disadvantage fairly."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The report provides a reasonably comprehensive risk and disadvantage section, with client-specific switching loss scenarios at three growth rates corroborated by the pension switch report (P10). The critical yield and cost premium figures are present. The employer contribution loss for People's Pension is explicitly stated. IHT legislative risk is flagged. Cancellation risks and liquidity delay are stated. Generic limitations on charges and cashflow assumptions are included.\n\nHowever, two material items identified in the supporting evidence are absent from the report's risk disclosures:\n\n1. The Standard Life Leaver Discount of 0.4800% (P15): The Standard Life policy information explicitly states the plan benefits from a Differential Fund Discount and that 'These low charges may not be available from other pension providers upon transfer.' This is a plan-specific benefit the client is giving up by transferring Standard Life. The report never mentions this discount or its loss. Under the 'benefits, features or protections the client is giving up' limb of this check, this is a material omission. The pension switch report recording 'No benefits were listed' does not override the underlying policy document's explicit disclosure — and the guards note that a research finding undercutting the recommendation is material information even if not a formal risk warning.\n\n2. The Standard Life charges comparison inconsistency (page 8 states 0.08% saving; page 11 table shows +0.26% increase, computed on the wrong fund value per the derived figures): The report presents contradictory information to the client on whether switching Standard Life is cheaper or more expensive. This undermines the client's ability to assess the disadvantage fairly, and is not resolved or disclosed as a limitation in the report.\n\nThe Zurich potential exit charge on capital units is noted in the Zurich policy information (P25) but the pension switch report's comparison summary (P26/P6) incorporates transfer penalties into the calculation ('The cost of any transfer penalties applied by the existing providers are included in the calculation'), which partially addresses this — though the report itself does not explicitly name the Zurich capital unit exit charge as a risk the client should understand before deciding.\n\nThe mid growth rate discrepancy (2.94% report narrative vs 2.95% switch report) is minor and may reflect rounding; it is noted but not itself a material concern.\n\nThe income drawdown risks from the Key Features are not relevant at this stage as the recommendation is accumulation.\n\nThe vulnerability overlay: the extraction notes vulnerability assessment outcomes are not present in the document. No vulnerability-specific risk disclosures are required that are absent beyond what is already identified.\n\nOn balance, the omission of the Standard Life Leaver Discount as a benefit being surrendered on transfer is a material