{"groupId":"G8.3","requirement":"Tax consequences of the recommendation are fairly, clearly, accurately and prominently explained in the suitability report and are consistent with the supporting evidence.","reportSays":"The report states the following tax consequences: (1) Pension tax relief: a net contribution of £80 becomes £100 gross. (2) Death benefits before age 75 paid free of tax; after age 75 taxed at beneficiary's marginal rate. (3) From 6 April 2027, most unused DC pensions and death benefits will be included in the taxable estate for IHT; exceptions include spouse/civil partner pensions, dependants' scheme pensions and death-in-service benefits. (4) Scottish Widows small pot encashment: 25% tax-free, 75% taxed at marginal rate, estimated net receipt £170 from a £200 fund withdrawal. (5) Alternative for Scottish Widows: if left to grow until retirement, full amount could potentially be taken within personal allowance with no tax due. (6) Flexi-access drawdown: up to 25% tax-free lump sum at crystallisation (subject to Lump Sum Allowance of £268,275); remainder taxed at marginal rate. (7) Annual allowance excess charge of up to 45%; tax relief limited to £3,600 p.a. or 100% of salary. (8) Benefits can be drawn from age 55 (rising to 57 from 6 April 2028) and advice should be taken to draw benefits tax-efficiently. (9) IHT currently £0 for the client; pensions currently outside estate.","fileSays":"The Key Features document [P2] confirms 25% tax-free lump sum entitlement, income tax payable on income and lump sum payments, and references the lump sum allowance and lump sum and death benefit allowance. [P4] confirms death before 75: benefits normally paid tax free; death after 75: benefits taxed at beneficiaries' marginal rate; lump sum to trust or personal representatives: 45% charge. [P6] confirms FAD 25% tax-free lump sum subject to Lump Sum Allowance of £268,275; remainder taxed at marginal rate; and the replacement of the lifetime allowance with new allowances. [P3] and [P5] confirm IHT liability currently £0 and the April 2027 IHT change for DC pensions. [P10] confirms the small pot encashment tax treatment verbatim: 25% tax-free, 75% at marginal rate, estimated £170 net. The pension switch research report [P23] shows the death benefit shortfall immediately after switch: existing £110,185.60 vs new solution day one £106,880.00, a shortfall of £3,305.00 — this is a material tax/financial consequence of switching (loss of immediate death benefit value equal to the advice charge) that is present in the research file. The suitability report does not explicitly explain that the immediate death benefit will fall by £3,305 on day one due to the initial advice charge being deducted from the fund on transfer. The report's IHT treatment of death benefits pre-/post-75 is consistent with [P4]. The report's Scottish Widows small pot tax treatment is consistent with [P10]. The report's FAD tax disclosure is consistent with [P6].","discrepancies":["The pension switch research report [P23] discloses a day-one death benefit shortfall of £3,305 (existing £110,185.60 vs new solution day one £106,880.60) arising from the initial advice charge. The suitability report does not explain this immediate reduction in death benefit value or its tax/financial consequence for beneficiaries, representing a material omission of a consequence identified in the supporting evidence.","The report's small pot tax calculation states 25% of a £200 fund = £50 tax-free, 75% = £150 taxed at marginal rate, net receipt estimated at £170. If the client is a basic rate taxpayer (20%), tax on £150 would be £30, giving £170 net — this arithmetic is consistent. However, the report does not state the client's marginal tax rate being applied to confirm the £170 estimate, making this client-specific calculation unverifiable from the report alone (though the guard acknowledges this is unverifiable rather than wrong).","The report states the Scottish Widows fund value as £211.32 in the existing arrangements table but refers to it as a '£200 pension fund withdrawal' in the tax consequence explanation — a rounding/approximation that is not explicitly flagged as an approximation.","The report describes the IHT change from 6 April 2027 in generic legislative terms without quantifying the potential IHT exposure on the client's pension estate (currently ~£110,185), though the report does confirm current IHT liability is £0."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The report covers the principal tax consequences of the recommendations competently: pension tax relief, FAD crystallisation tax treatment, death benefit tax treatment pre- and post-75, the IHT legislative change from April 2027, the small pot encashment tax outcome, and the annual allowance. These are corroborated by [P2], [P4], [P6], [P3], [P5] and [P10]. The FAD disclosure in [P6] matches the report's assertion verbatim. The death benefit tax treatment in [P4] is consistent with the report's assertion. The small pot tax treatment in [P10] matches the report exactly. The guard on generic legislative explanation vs client-specific consequence is relevant to the IHT disclosure, which is legislative in nature; however, the report does confirm the client's current IHT liability is £0 and that pensions are currently outside the estate, which provides some personalisation. The more significant gap is the day-one death benefit shortfall of £3,305 disclosed in [P23]. This is a direct financial consequence of the initial advice charge being extracted from the fund at the point of transfer, reducing the death benefit available to beneficiaries immediately. The suitability report discloses the initial advice fee of £3,305.55 [P7] but does not connect this to its effect on the death benefit value on day one. This is a material disadvantage/risk identified in the supporting evidence that is not explained in the report. The omission is particularly relevant given the client's age (69) and the short window before the April 2027 IHT change. The small pot rounding (£211.32 described as '£200') is a minor presentation issue. The marginal rate gap is noted but the guard correctly identifies this as unverifiable rather than wrong. On balance, the main substantive gap is the death benefit shortfall consequence.","citations":[{"passageId":"P10","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Under the small pot rules, a £200 pension fund withdrawal would be taxed with 25% taken as tax-free cash and the remaining 75% taxed as income at your marginal tax rate. We estimate you would receive £170 net.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"An individual can take up to 25% of the pension fund being crystallised as a tax-free lump sum at outset (subject to the Lump Sum Allowance of £268,275 for people with no form of lifetime allowance protection). The remainder of the fund remains invested within a tax advantaged environment from which the individual can take regular or ad-hoc income withdrawals to suit their needs and circumstances. There is no upper limit to the amount the individual can withdraw as an income. However, each withdrawal is taxed at their marginal rate of Income Tax.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Key Features (1).md","category":"H","quote":"If you die before you're 75, we'll normally pay any benefits tax free to your nominated beneficiaries, at the trustees' discretion (unless you have an Aviva Pension Trust, in which case the rules of that Trust will apply).","cells":null},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Key Features (1).md","category":"H","quote":"You can normally withdraw up to 25% of your pension fund as a tax-free lump sum. You may have to pay income tax on any income payments and lump sum payments you take. Both are treated as income, and therefore the tax you pay will depend on your personal circumstances.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P23","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Existing pension:","£110,185.60","New solution day one:","£106,880.60","Shortfall in death benefit immediately after the switch:","£3,305.00"]},{"passageId":"P3","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Currently, your Inheritance Tax liability will be £0. We will continue to monitor your IHT position at our future reviews.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P26","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Pensions are currently outside of your estate for IHT purposes, however from 6 April 2027, most unused defined contribution pensions and death benefits (such as drawdown funds and uncrystallised pots) will be included in your taxable estate for IHT.","cells":null}],"severity":"Moderate","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

