{"groupId":"G8.1","requirement":"Material disadvantages, risk factors and limitations of the recommendation are fairly, clearly, accurately and prominently disclosed in the suitability report, consistent with the supporting evidence.","reportSays":"The report discloses multiple material disadvantages and limitations: (1) including adviser fees, the Aviva plan is 4.24% more expensive than current plans, requiring £4,672 outperformance in year one; (2) at mid growth rate of 2.94%, the plan must grow at 4.8% p.a. to age 75 to match current plan value; (3) switching effect at low growth = £10,400 / 9.6% decrease; at mid growth = £12,000 / 9.4% decrease; at high growth = £15,000 / 10.0% decrease in projected value at age 75; (4) loss of employer contributions if People's Pension transferred; (5) recommended Aviva funds have not outperformed Zurich funds over five years; (6) Zurich funds allocated to higher-risk funds more susceptible to market volatility; (7) market movement risk during transfer pending period; (8) no guarantee new investment will outperform existing; (9) liquidity delay of 10-15 working days; (10) Standard Life plan 0.08% more expensive than recommended solution (per recommendation rationale); (11) tax on encashment of Scottish Widows small pot (25% TFC, 75% taxed at marginal rate, estimated net £170); (12) alternative: if left to grow, full amount could be taken within personal allowance tax-free; (13) transaction costs excluded from charges; (14) charge figure disclaimer — monetary terms may differ from illustration, annual recurring charges assume no growth; (15) Standard Life performance data unavailable; (16) cashflow assumes all surplus income spent. On a like-for-like basis (excluding adviser fee), the Aviva plan is stated as 0.24% less expensive / £264 underperformance tolerance.","fileSays":"The Defaqto pension switching report (P1/P2) provides switching effect figures for the three constituent plans. For the Aviva plan alone: at low growth, effect of switching is £10 or 0.3% decrease; at high growth, £10 or 0.2% decrease. The Zurich Policy Info (P39/P40/P42) flags that some plans have guaranteed annuity rates, exit charges, and that life cover/waiver benefits are no longer available on new plans — the transfer checklist prompts consideration of features that may be lost. P42 explicitly confirms for the Zurich plan: 'Guarantees, bonuses, guaranteed minimum pension, safeguarding, life styling, block/bulk transfers, market value reduction, protected or enhanced tax free cash do not apply to this plan.' The Aviva Key Features (P4/P7) disclose standard pension risks: value can go down as well as up, client could get back less than invested, income drawdown risks including depletion of fund if high income taken. Aviva T&Cs (P9) confirm switching may expose client to adverse market movements during disinvestment period. The policy research (P10/P23/P27) shows transfer values and plan features. The fact find (P35/P36) shows client objective is to consolidate pensions and retire in approximately 2 years with £1,700 net/month household income. The internal inconsistencies noted in the extraction indicate: (a) the Standard Life charges table (page 11) shows +0.26% impact (an increase) while page 8 narrative says the Standard Life plan is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution' — these are contradictory; (b) the EA5 charge rows in the canonical model are computed on Zurich's fund value (£103,439) not Standard Life's (£3,002), making the Standard Life comparison unreliable; (c) the critical yield table labels mid growth rate as 2.95% while page 11 narrative says 2.94%.","discrepancies":["The report states the Standard Life plan is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution' (page 8 narrative), implying switching saves 0.08% for Standard Life. However, the charges comparison table (page 11) shows the impact of the recommended alternative for Standard Life as +0.26% — an increase, not a saving. These figures are contradictory.","The EA5 (Standard Life) charge rows in the canonical model are computed on Zurich's fund value (£103,439.24) rather than Standard Life's own value (£3,002.00), meaning the Standard Life charge comparison figures are calculated on the wrong plan and are arithmetically unreliable.","The report states mid growth rate as 2.94% in the page 11 narrative but 2.95% in the critical yield table on page 18 — a minor inconsistency in the same document.","The switching effect figures in the report (£10,400/9.6% low; £12,000/9.4% mid; £15,000/10.0% high) relate to the combined three-plan switch. The Defaqto report (P1/P2) provides per-plan switching effects (e.g. Aviva alone: £10/0.3% decrease at low growth) but the aggregate figures are not directly verifiable from the passages retrieved — the report presents combined figures without clearly reconciling them against the per-plan research tool outputs.","The Zurich transfer checklist (P39/P40) raises the question of features that may be lost on transfer (life cover, waiver, guaranteed annuity rates). P42 confirms these do not apply to the Zurich plan. However, the extraction notes that featuresOrGuaranteesLost is absent from the report for all five existing arrangements (EA1–EA5) — the report does not contain a section explicitly addressing features or guarantees lost for each plan being switched, even though the Zurich transfer documentation prompts this consideration.","The report presents both a like-for-like cost saving (0.24% less expensive / £264) and an including-adviser-fee cost increase (4.24% more expensive / £4,672) without clearly reconciling them for the reader, creating a risk that the materially adverse adviser-fee impact is not sufficiently prominent relative to the headline cost saving.","The derived figures show the required outperformance of £4,672 at 4.24% implies a fund value of £110,188.68, which does not match any stated arrangement value or sum — a minor arithmetic inconsistency in the report's own figures, though immaterial in magnitude.","The loss scenarios (£10,400/9.6%; £12,000/9.4%; £15,000/10.0%) imply fund values (£108,333; £127,659; £150,000 respectively) that match no stated arrangement value — the base figures used for these projections are not fully transparent in the report."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The report contains a substantial and largely client-specific risk and disadvantage disclosure. Multiple quantified loss scenarios at three growth rates are presented, the cost disadvantage including adviser fees is clearly stated with a monetary figure, the critical yield requirement is given, performance shortfall versus Zurich is acknowledged, tax consequences of the Scottish Widows encashment are explained with a net receipt estimate, and the People's Pension employer contribution risk is identified. These are material disclosures that meet a significant part of the requirement.\n\nHowever, several concerns remain after applying the false-positive guards:\n\n1. STANDARD LIFE CHARGE CONTRADICTION: The report simultaneously states the Standard Life plan is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution' (page 8, implying switching saves cost) and the charges table shows +0.26% impact for Standard Life (implying switching costs more). The derived figures confirm the EA5 rows are computed on Zurich's fund value, not Standard Life's £3,002. This means the cost justification for the Standard Life switch — one of three plans being consolidated — is internally contradicted and arithmetically based on the wrong fund. This is a material accuracy failure for that specific recommendation limb, directly relevant to the disadvantages disclosure.\n\n2. FEATURES/GUARANTEES LOST: The extraction confirms featuresOrGuaranteesLost is absent (NotPresentInDocument) for all five existing arrangements. The Zurich transfer documentation (P39/P40) explicitly prompts consideration of whether features such as guaranteed annuity rates, life cover, and waiver benefits are present and would be lost. P42 confirms these do not apply to the Zurich plan specifically. However, the report does not contain an explicit features-lost analysis for any plan. The guard notes that disadvantages may be spread across sections — the report does address lifestyling funds being left behind (for Standard Life), employer contributions (for People's Pension), and higher-risk fund exposure (for Zurich) — so this is partially mitigated. But a structured features-lost assessment is absent from the report in the way the Zurich provider documentation contemplates.\n\n3. PROMINENCE OF ADVERSE COST IMPACT: The like-for-like saving (0.24%/£264) and the adviser-fee-inclusive cost increase (4.24%/£4,672) are both stated but the difference between £264 and £4,672 is stark. The adviser-fee-inclusive figure is disclosed in the disadvantages section, which is appropriate, but the presentation of both figures without clear reconciliation carries a risk of consumer confusion. This is a moderate presentation concern rather than an omission.\n\n4. VULNERABILITY OVERLAY: The fact find (P35) records the client as a private tenant, aged approximately 69 (date of birth context from P42 suggests mid-50s but the cashflow references ages 69/65 for male/female life expectancy — P26). The client has a monthly deficit of £288 (P32: expenditure £1,718 vs income £1,430) and is expecting to retire in approximately 2 years. This financial fragility makes the accuracy of the cost comparison for the Standard Life switch particularly material — a client in this position needs reliable cost information to make an informed decision about each consolidation. The contradiction in the Standard Life charges is therefore of heightened concern given the client's circumstances.\n\n5. The minor growth rate inconsistency (2.94% vs 2.95%) and the base-fund-value transparency issues in the loss scenarios are low-severity presentation matters.\n\nOverall: the report makes a genuine and substantial attempt at disadvantage disclosure, but the Standard Life charge contradiction (0.08% saving stated vs +0.26% increase shown in the table, computed on the wrong fund value) is a material accuracy failure in the disadvantages section for that recommendation. Combined with the absence of an explicit features-lost analysis and the prominence concern around the adviser-fee cost impact, the outcome is Potential Concern at Moderate severity — the Standard Life issue alone would justify this; the other matters reinforce it.","citations":[{"passageId":"P28","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"The plan itself is 0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution","cells":null},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Effect of switching","£10 or 0.3% decrease","Low growth rate"]},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Effect of switching","-","£10 or 0.2% decrease","High growth rate – (5.88%)"]},{"passageId":"P39","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"Both life cover and waiver benefits are no longer available on new pension plans. You would also need to complete a medical questionnaire to take out similar cover separate to your new pension plan.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P42","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"Guarantees, bonuses, guaranteed minimum pension, safeguarding, life styling, block/bulk transfers, market value reduction, protected or enhanced tax free cash do not apply to this plan.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P40","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"How do the charges compare? When you transfer your pension, you may find that the new plan has lower charges but this is not always the case.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P9","source":"Terms and conditions (1).md","category":"H","quote":"There may therefore be incidences where You are disinvested for a period and subject to potentially adverse market movements, We will not be liable for any losses arising during this period.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P7","source":"Key Features (1).md","category":"H","quote":"The value of your pension pot and the retirement benefits it can provide may go down as well as up. You could get back less than has been invested.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P20","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"they have not outperformed your current Zurich funds. It's important to note that your Zurich investments are allocated to higher-risk funds, which are more susceptible to market volatility, resulting in greater fluctuations in value.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P26","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"I have completed a stress test based on if an immediate market crash were to occur in combination with the increased inflation level. This showed that if your plan sustained an immediate loss of -30% and the increased inflation continued, there would be no shortfall that requires your pension or investment funds to be used.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P32","source":"Fact Find (Test 1).md","category":"B","quote":null,"cells":["Total Net Monthly Income","£ 1,430.00"]},{"passageId":"P32","source":"Fact Find (Test 1).md","category":"B","quote":null,"cells":["Total Monthly Expenditure","£ 1,718.00"]},{"passageId":"P32","source":"Fact Find (Test 1).md","category":"B","quote":null,"cells":["Total Monthly Disposable Income","£ -288.00"]}],"severity":"Moderate","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

