s all funds within 20-60% equities range are more expensive than Aviva offering. Pension switch report (G) confirms the three plans switched are Aviva, Zurich and Standard Life to Aviva Pension Portfolio Choice SIPP. Zurich policy info (E/P3) confirms: 'Guarantees, bonuses, guaranteed minimum pension, safeguarding, life styling, block/bulk transfers, market value reduction, protected or enhanced tax free cash do not apply to this plan.' Aviva policy info (E/P8) confirms: 'This plan doesn't have any guaranteed rates, including annuity rates' and 'The plan doesn't include a Guaranteed Minimum Pension'; plan has Lifestage Investment Approach (auto-switching). Scottish Widows (E/P10, P14) confirms: 'Money4Life Plans (Plans starting with ZU) do not support ongoing advisor charging, the transferring of Servicing Rights or Commission.' Standard Life (E/P9): transfer value available, no safeguarded benefits evident from retrieved passages but no explicit confirmation in retrieved text that no guarantees exist. Pension switch report (G/P6) shows a product comparison ranking: New Solution ranked 5th (£125,000 maturity value) versus Existing Solution ranked 2nd (£127,000). The like-for-like charges comparison (P22) shows Standard Life impact as +0.26% (an increase, not a saving). The report's page 8 narrative states Standard Life is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution' (i.e. a saving), directly contradicting the +0.26% shown in the charges table on page 11.","discrepancies":["Standard Life cost claim contradiction: report page 8 states the existing Standard Life plan is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution' (implying switching saves 0.08%), but the charges comparison table on page 11 shows the impact of the recommended alternative for Standard Life as +0.26% — meaning the new plan is MORE expensive for Standard Life, not less. These are irreconcilable.","EA5 charge arithmetic error: derived figures confirm the EA5 annual charge of £186.19 at 0.18% implies a fund value of £103,438.89 (Zurich's value), not Standard Life's £3,002 — meaning the Standard Life row in the charges table was computed on the wrong plan's fund value, rendering any cost comparison for Standard Life unreliable.","New solution ranks 5th out of compared products (£125,000 maturity value) while the existing solution ranks 2nd (£127,000) in the pension switch report — the recommended solution produces a lower projected maturity value than the existing solution and several alternatives, which is not addressed or reconciled in the report's net benefit conclusions.","Net benefit conclusion for Standard Life (page 8) claims 'lower costs' as a benefit, but the charges table directly contradicts this for that specific plan.","No explicit confirmation in the retrieved evidence that Standard Life has no safeguarded benefits or guarantees — the Standard Life policy info passage (P9) does not contain an explicit statement that no guarantees exist, unlike the Aviva and Zurich provider documentation which expressly state this.","The report states the net benefit for Zurich switching includes 'lower costs' indirectly (via consolidation), but the Zurich rationale section (page 8) does not state a specific cost saving percentage for Zurich while the charges table shows -0.67% — this is not a direct contradiction but an omission in the narrative.","People's Pension retained rationale (employer contributions) is supported by evidence, no discrepancy there.","Scottish Widows encashment rationale (no adviser management, no suitable single fund) is corroborated by provider documentation confirming no adviser charging — no discrepancy."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The replacement rationale is partially supported but contains a material internal contradiction and an arithmetic error that together undermine the Standard Life switching justification. The most serious issue is the Standard Life cost claim: the report's page 8 narrative says switching saves 0.08% (existing plan is 0.08% more expensive), while the charges comparison table on page 11 shows +0.26% — the recommended plan is actually more expensive for Standard Life. The derived figures confirm this is not a rounding issue but an arithmetic error: the EA5 charge row was computed using Zurich's fund value (£103,439) rather than Standard Life's (£3,002), corrupting the entire Standard Life cost comparison. This means one of the stated net benefit claims ('lower costs') for Standard Life is contradicted by the report's own charges table. This is within the highest/high severity band per the decision logic (a switch justified on cost savings that the report's own comparison table contradicts). The pension switch report (G/P6) also shows the new solution ranks 5th with a projected maturity value of £125,000 versus the existing solution's £127,000 — a £2,000 or 1.6% decrease. The report does not address why switching to a solution with a lower projected maturity value than the existing arrangement represents a net benefit, other than through qualitative claims about flexibility and oversight. These qualitative benefits (greater investment flexibility, adviser management, consolidation) are corroborated by file notes in category C and provider documentation (SW confirming no adviser charging, Aviva confirming lifestage approach, Zurich confirming no suitable risk-5 funds). The Scottish Widows encashment rationale is well-supported: provider documentation confirms no adviser charging capability. The People's Pension retention rationale (ongoing employer contributions) is stated and not contradicted. The Aviva switch rationale (lifestyling outside risk range, no suitable fund at ATR 5) is corroborated by Aviva policy info confirming the Lifestage Investment Approach and file notes confirming the only mixed asset Aviva fund is rated 4. The Zurich switch rationale is supported by file notes. No vulnerability factors are identified in the evidence that would materially alter this assessment. The Standard Life cost contradiction and the absence of explicit confirmation of no safeguarded benefits for Standard Life (unlike Aviva and Zurich where provider letters expressly confirm this) are the residual concerns. On balance, the Standard Life cost contradiction is a genuine finding not explained by any guard: the guard requires the contradiction to arise from a different charge basis, but here both figures purport to be like-for-like annual recurring charge comparisons for the same plan. The lower projected maturity value for the new solution is a concern that is not addressed in the rationale.","citations":[{"passageId":"P22","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Standard Life","0.18%","£186.19","+0.26%","£268.94"]},{"passageId":"P22","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"By switching to this scheme, you will benefit from lower costs, greater investment flexibility, improved retirement options, and continued professional oversight, ensuring your pension remains on track to support your long-term financial goals.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["New solution","0.6%","£125,000"]},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Existing Solution","0.3%","£127,000"]},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["New solution","-£2,000 or -1.6% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P10","source":"SW Further Info 4.md","category":"E","quote":"Money4Life Plans (Plans starƟng with ZU) do not support ongoing advisor charging, the transferring of Servicing Rights or Commission.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P14","source":"Scottish Widows Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"Money4Life Plans (Plans starting with ZU) do not support ongoing advisor charging, the transferring of