aining why the superior-performing Zurich-only alternative was rejected in favour of the lower-returning consolidation is present in the suitability report.","The mid growth rate is referred to as 2.94% on page 11 of the suitability report (P18) but the pension switch report table labels it 2.95% (P4), a minor inconsistency.","The report's costComparison paths for EA1 through EA5 are all absent (NotPresentInDocument per extraction metadata), meaning no plan-specific cost comparison is documented for EA1 (People's Pension, retained) or EA2 (Scottish Widows, encashed)."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The central concern under G9.8 is whether higher costs are justified by the additional benefits claimed. Several material issues arise.\n\n1. Standard Life cost contradiction (High severity trigger): The report's own charges table (P14) shows the recommended plan is +0.26% more expensive than Standard Life on a like-for-like basis, yet the recommendation narrative (P15) states the existing Standard Life plan is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution'. These directly contradict each other. The derived figures confirm the Standard Life rows in the charges table are computed on Zurich's fund value (~£103,439) rather than Standard Life's £3,002, meaning the +0.26%/+£268.94 figures themselves are unreliable — but the narrative claim of a 0.08% saving is equally unsupported by any correctly-computed figure. The net benefit conclusion for Standard Life then asserts 'lower costs' as a benefit when neither a properly-computed saving nor a correctly-attributed cost comparison is available. This is a material discrepancy that the guards do not explain away.\n\n2. Overall cost increase: Including adviser fees, the report openly acknowledges the new plan is 4.24% more expensive, requiring £4,672 outperformance in year one and a 4.8% critical yield. The pension switch report (P4) shows the all-in switch produces lower projected maturity values at every growth rate. The assertion that 4.8% is achievable based on past performance is not corroborated by any quantified fund performance evidence in the pack; the Zurich appendix (P12) shows N/A for all statistics, and the pension switch report's own comparison ranks the new solution 5th (P8). The 'higher costs justified by benefits' claim therefore rests on an unsubstantiated performance assertion for the largest plan.\n\n3. Features and guarantees: No featuresOrGuaranteesLost analysis appears anywhere in the report for any ceding plan. The Zurich policy document (P9) explicitly raises guaranteed annuity rates as a question but the checkbox is not ticked, leaving it unresolved. The guard requires provider documentation confirming none exist before this can be cleared; none is present.\n\n4. Generic benefits: 'Greater investment choice' and 'adviser charging facilitation' are asserted for multiple plans without evidence that these features are unavailable on the ceding plans. The guard states a 'better fund choice' benefit needs a fund count or named client requirement to count. For Zurich this requirement is partly met — the report specifically states Zurich lacks risk level 5 funds (P19), which is a client-specific need. For Aviva and Standard Life the assertion is generic.\n\n5. Alternative not addressed: The pension switch report (P8) shows retaining Zurich alone is the highest-ranking option (£128,000 at mid growth). The report does not explain why this superior-return alternative was rejected. While consolidation is cited as a client priority (P19), the report does not quantify what the client sacrifices by consolidating rather than retaining Zurich.\n\nThe vulnerability overlay: no specific vulnerability characteristics are identified in the evidence pack as materially affecting this assessment.\n\nOn balance, the Standard Life cost claim directly contradicts the charges table, the features/guarantees question is unresolved for Zurich, and the justification for higher overall costs (4.24% including adviser fees) is not corroborated by evidence of fund performance. These are not explained by any guard. The outcome is Potential Concern at High severity.","citations":[{"passageId":"P14","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Standard Life","0.18%","£186.19","+0.26%","+£268.94"]},{"passageId":"P15","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Lower Costs - The receiving scheme has a lower overall cost structure compared to your existing pension, meaning more of your savings remain invested for growth rather than being lost to charges. The plan itself is 0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution","cells":null},{"passageId":"P18","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"the recommended plan would need to grow by 4.8% per year up to age 75 in order to meet the same value as your current plan. Based on the past performance of your recommended funds, I do believe that this is achievable.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Mid growth rate – (2.95%)","Existing pension £127,000","New solution £115,000","Effect of switching £12,000 or 9.4% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P8","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["1","Zurich - Zurich pension","0.2%","£128,000","2","Existing Solution","0.3%","£127,000","5","New Solution","0.6%","£125,000"]},{"passageId":"P9","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"Guaranteed rates? - Some of our plans have guaranteed annuity rates when you retire which may give you a higher pension than you could get based on current rates.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P5","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"Transfer value and early retirement charge — There are no deductions taken from the current value if benefits are transferred or taken.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P19","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Zurich does not offer risk level 5 funds, so to achieve this profile, a blend of a risk level 7 and a risk level 2 fund has been used.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P12","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Zurich - Zurich pension","Statistics","N/A","N/A","N/A"]}],"severity":"High","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

