{"groupId":"G9.5","requirement":"Switching costs exceed the benefits: the report must demonstrate that the costs of switching do not exceed the benefits, with a comparison of charges, an assessment of safeguarded benefits or features lost, and evidence supporting why any net cost increase is justified.","reportSays":"The report asserts: (1) No exit penalties on any of the three ceding plans (Aviva, Zurich, Standard Life). (2) For Aviva (EA3): switching saves 0.06% pa (−£2.25 pa). (3) For Zurich (EA4): switching saves 0.49% pa (−£506.85 pa). (4) For Standard Life (EA5): the report narrative on p8 states the plan is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution' (i.e. a saving of 0.08%), yet the charges comparison table on p11 records the impact as +0.26% / +£268.94 pa — meaning the new plan is MORE expensive for Standard Life. (5) On a like-for-like basis across all three, the new Aviva plan is 0.24% less expensive (saving £264 pa); including adviser fees, the new plan is 4.24% more expensive (£4,672 additional cost pa). (6) Benefits cited: lower costs, avoidance of lifestyling funds, greater investment choice, adviser charging facilitation, ongoing monitoring, and multi-asset fund selection aligned to agreed risk level 5. (7) Critical yields stated as 1.9% (low), 4.8% (mid), 7.8% (high) — these are corroborated by the switch report. (8) Features/guarantees lost: the report does not address this (confirmed absent by extraction metadata for all five paths). (9) Out-of-market risk disclosed. (10) Scottish Widows (EA2) encashment: net receipt ~£170 under small pots rules (25% TFC, 75% at marginal rate); or full amount potentially tax-free if left to retirement. No exit penalty stated.","fileSays":"The Defaqto Pension Switch Report (passages P1–P8) shows: (1) No exit penalties recorded for the three ceding plans (consistent with report). (2) For the overall switch, the existing pension day-one value is £110,185.60 and the new solution day-one value is £106,880.60 —