{"groupId":"G9.1","requirement":"Where the Suitability Report contains a recommendation to replace, switch, transfer, consolidate, encash or surrender an existing product or investment, is the comparison of existing versus recommended product and investments consistent with and supported by the evidence provided?","reportSays":"The report recommends switching Aviva (SP50163762), Zurich (P10026-719-001/DL) and Standard Life (D2301334000) into a new Aviva Pension Portfolio on the platform, encashing Scottish Widows (ZU4225575) and retaining People's Pension (6197175/PP). For each switching plan the report provides a comparison across dimensions including fund range, risk alignment, retirement options, adviser servicing, charges and performance. For Scottish Widows (EA2) the report states 29 funds available, no single multi-asset fund aligned to risk 5, does not allow adviser management, and limits retirement options to full and partial UFPLS only. For Aviva (EA3) the report states existing 0.50% fund charge / £18.72 total annual recurring, recommended 0.44% total / 0.06% cheaper; lifestyling fund (risk 2) moving outside agreed risk range; no adviser servicing; 80+ funds vs 6,900+ recommended. For Zurich (EA4) the report states existing 0.93% / £961.98; lifestyling concern; no adviser servicing; full/partial UFPLS only vs FAD on new plan; 60+ funds. For Standard Life (EA5) the report states the existing plan is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution'; lifestyling; 300 funds; FAD only. Charges comparison table on page 11 shows Standard Life impact as +0.26% (an increase, not a saving). The report states no safeguarded features exist for any plan. A pension switching report (Defaqto) is referenced showing cost/benefit analysis, critical yield and alternative comparisons. The report acknowledges that including adviser fees the new plan needs to outperform by £4,672 (4.24%) in year one, and at mid-growth rate of 2.94% the recommended plan must grow at 4.8% p.a. to age 75 to match the current plan.","fileSays":"Provider documentation (Aviva [P18/P40], Scottish Widows [P25/P33/P46], Zurich [P30/P31/P34/P48]) and policy research [P1/P8/P13/P35] confirm: (1) Aviva SP50163762 has no safeguarded benefits, no GAR, no GMP, no exit charge, no adviser charging, auto-switching lifestage approach, 0.50% annual fund charge, retirement options FAD and UFPLS [P18/P40]; (2) Scottish Widows ZU4225575 has no safeguarded benefits, no GAR, no GMP, no exit penalties, does not support ongoing adviser charging or transfer of servicing rights, retirement options full and partial UFPLS only, 29 funds [P25/P33/P46]; (3) Zurich P10026-719-001/DL has no safeguarded benefits, no GAR, no GMP confirmed [P48]; drawdown not available on this plan [P48/P34]; potential exit charge on capital units [P34]; (4) Standard Life D2301334000 allows adviser charging [P8/P23]; retirement options FAD only [P23]. The Defaqto pension switching report [P55/P3/P4/P9/P10/P17/P20] covers all three switching plans (Aviva, Zurich, Standard Life) with projected maturity values, reduction in yield, critical yield, effect of switching and alternative comparisons. It confirms initial adviser charge of £3,305 reduces day-one fund from £110,185.60 to £106,880.60 [P12]. Benefits lost section for the overall switch states 'No benefits were listed for the existing products' [P17]. The new solution investment details show CT Universal MAP Balanced Fund C Acc and HSBC Global Strategy Balanced Portfolio C, each 50% [P12]. Performance data in the suitability report [P14] shows Aviva My Future Focus Consolidation 5yr 15.58%, Aviva Risk 5 portfolio 5yr 40.02% [P14], and Zurich Managed Pn AP 5yr 44.32% [P6/P32] — the report acknowledges the recommended funds have not outperformed Zurich. Policy research [P35] confirms Zurich is invested in lifestyle strategy 'No' (Managed AP accumulation), contradicting the report's lifestyling claim for Zurich. Charges table in the switching report [P55] and the internal inconsistency noted in extraction shows Standard Life impact as +0.26% while the suitability report narrative states 0.08% saving — these are contradictory. The Defaqto new-solution selection ranking [P9] shows the new solution (rank 5, £125,000) ranks below the existing solution (rank 2, £127,000), Zurich-only (rank 1, £128,000), Aviva-only (rank 3, £126,000) and Standard Life-only (rank 4, £126,000) on mid-growth maturity value. Exit charge on Zurich capital units is confirmed possible [P34] but the report does not address this. People's Pension is a live workplace scheme with active employer contributions (£897.13 p.a.) and is retained [P16]; no employer contribution loss issue arises for the switched plans.","discrepancies":["1. Standard Life charges contradiction: the report narrative (page 8) states the Standard Life plan is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution' (i.e. switching saves 0.08%), but the charges comparison table in the report/switching report shows the impact as +0.26% — meaning the new solution is MORE expensive for Standard Life, not cheaper. These are directly contradictory figures within the report itself, corroborated by the derived figures confirming the EA5 charge row is computed on Zurich's fund value not Standard Life's.","2. Zurich lifestyling claim: the report states Zurich funds are 'currently invested in lifestyling funds, which automatically reduce investment risk as you approach retirement', but the policy research [P35] records the Zurich plan as 'Invested in lifestyle strategy: No' and the fund held is 'Managed AP (accumulation)', which is not a lifestyling fund.","3. Zurich exit charge: the Zurich policy information [P34] confirms that if the plan has capital units, an exit charge applies on transfer. The report does not disclose or address this potential exit charge.","4. New solution maturity value ranking: the Defaqto switching report [P9] ranks the recommended new solution 5th out of 9 options by mid-growth maturity value (£125,000), below the existing solution (£127,000) and several alternatives including Zurich-only and Aviva-only. The report does not explain why a solution ranking below the existing arrangement and several alternatives was selected, beyond non-cost reasons.","5. EA5 (Standard Life) charge row computed on wrong fund value: derived figures confirm that the EA5 ongoing charge of £186.19 at 0.18% and impact of £268.94 at 0.26% both imply a fund value of approximately £103,439 — Zurich's value — rather than Standard Life's £3,002. This means the entire cost comparison for Standard Life is unreliable.","6. Scottish Widows fund range: the report states 29 funds available for Scottish Widows; this is consistent with policy research [P23] and provider documentation.","7. Performance chart label swap: the suitability report's two charts on page 13 swap the A/D labels between Aviva and Zurich, creating confusion about which fund's performance is being shown, though the underlying performance figures (15.58% Aviva, 44.32% Zurich) are each corroborated individually in the passages.","8. The report states the recommended funds have 'not outperformed your current Zurich funds' and acknowledges this, but presents this as acceptable on risk/volatility grounds. This is consistent with the evidence but represents a known disadvantage of the switch for Zurich that is addressed only briefly.","9. Critical yield mid-growth rate stated as 2.94% in the report narrative [P29] but the switching report table labels it as 2.95% [P17] — marginal inconsistency."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The comparison is substantially evidenced. Provider documentation confirms the absence of safeguarded benefits across all five plans, corroborating the report's statements on that point. The Defaqto switching report provides plan-by-plan cost/benefit analysis, critical yield tables, alternative comparisons and a stakeholder comparison, satisfying the structural requirements for a switch analysis. The People's Pension is retained and has active employer contributions; since it is not being switched, no employer contribution loss issue arises for the switched plans.\n\nHowever, there are three material concerns:\n\n(A) The Standard Life cost claim is internally contradictory and arithmetically unsound. The report narrative states the existing Standard Life plan is '0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution' (a cost saving argument), but the charges comparison table shows +0.26% impact (the new solution is more expensive). The derived figures confirm the EA5 charge rows are computed using Zurich's fund value (c.£103,439) rather than Standard Life's own fund value (£3,002), rendering the entire Standard Life cost comparison unreliable. Since cost saving is a stated justification for the Standard Life switch, this is a material finding that goes to the heart of one switching rationale. This satisfies the 'High' severity threshold: a switch justified on cost savings that the report's own comparison table contradicts.\n\n(B) The report's lifestyling justification for the Zurich switch is contradicted by the policy research, which records the Zurich plan as not invested in a lifestyle strategy. The fund held (Managed AP accumulation) is not a lifestyling fund. This undermines one of the stated rationales for switching the Zurich plan, though the remaining rationales (no FAD, no adviser servicing, no multi-asset risk-5 fund, blended fund approach) are individually supported by evidence.\n\n(C) The potential Zurich exit charge on capital units is disclosed in provider documentation but not addressed in the report. This is a switching cost that should have been disclosed to the client.\n\nThe performance label swap between the two charts is a presentation issue rather than a substantive error, as the underlying figures are corroborated. The 2.94%/2.95% mid-growth rate discrepancy is minor. The new solution ranking below alternatives is acknowledged implicitly through the non-cost benefit comparison but not explicitly addressed; however, the switching report does include this comparison so the adviser had sight of it.\n\nVulnerability overlay: no specific vulnerability indicators are identified in the evidence that would materially alter this assessment.\n\nOverall: the Standard Life cost contradiction and the Standard Life charge row being computed on the wrong fund value are material; the Zurich lifestyling misstatement and the undisclosed Zurich exit charge are additional concerns. Outcome: Potential Concern at High severity.","citations":[{"passageId":"P47","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Lower Costs - The receiving scheme has a lower overall cost structure compared to your existing pension, meaning more of your savings remain invested for growth rather than being lost to charges. The plan itself is 0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution","cells":null},{"passageId":"P55","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Standard Life - SL pension","Personal Pension Plan","14/10/2025","None"]},{"passageId":"P35","source":"Test Case 1 - Policy Research -2025.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Invested in lifestyle strategy","No","No","1","5"]},{"passageId":"P34","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"If this plan has capital units, there will be an exit charge applied if the fund is transferred. This exit charge is a standard term of the contract.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P9","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["5","New Solution","0.6%","£125,000"]},{"passageId":"P17","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":"No benefits were listed for the existing products","cells":null},{"passageId":"P48","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"Drawdown options are not available on this plan; however, it can be transferred to another provider to do this.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P48","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"Guarantees, bonuses, guaranteed minimum pension, safeguarding, life styling, block/bulk transfers, market value reduction, protected or enhanced tax free cash do not apply to this plan.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P40","source":"Aviva Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"Adviser charging\nThis plan does not allow adviser charging.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P46","source":"Scottish Widows Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"Money4Life Plans (Plans starting with ZU) do not support ongoing advisor charging, the transferring of Servicing Rights or Commission.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P18","source":"Aviva Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"This plan doesn't have any guaranteed rates, including annuity rates","cells":null},{"passageId":"P12","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Existing pension:","£110,185.60"]},{"passageId":"P12","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["New solution day one:","£106,880.60"]},{"passageId":"P29","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Based on a mid-growth rate of 2.94%, including our advice fees, the recommended plan would need to grow by 4.8% per year up to age 75 in order to meet the same value as your current plan.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P38","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Multi-asset fund selection - Zurich does not offer risk level 5 funds, so to achieve this profile, a blend of a risk level 7 and a risk level 2 fund has been used.","cells":null}],"severity":"High","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

