y any passage in the evidence pack.","4. Aggregate combined pension value inconsistency: the report states 'approximately £110,000' on p3 for all five plans, but the arithmetic sum of the five individual values is £116,998.47; the figure £110,185 represents only the three switching plans, not all five.","5. Mid growth rate label inconsistency: p11 narrative states 2.94% mid growth rate; the pension switch report [P6] labels mid growth rate as 2.95%. These differ by 0.01%.","6. The like-for-like aggregate saving of 0.24% (£264) versus the all-in cost increase of 4.24% (£4,672) are both stated in the report without clear reconciliation, creating a materially misleading impression if only one figure is read.","7. No per-plan cost/benefit analysis is evidenced for EA5 (Standard Life) that is computed on Standard Life's own £3,002 fund value — the charge comparison row for EA5 uses Zurich's value throughout.","8. No features-or-guarantees analysis is present in the report for any of the five plans (confirmed as NotPresentInDocument by the extraction metadata for all five paths).","9. Standard Life contribution history [P16] shows employer payments, raising a question whether ongoing employer contributions could be lost on transfer; the report does not address this."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Per-plan corroboration of the -0.06%, -0.49%, and +0.26% charge differentials from an independent source (the switch report passages do not present an explicit per-plan charges table matching these figures)","Corroboration of the £170 net receipt and the £211.32/£200 tax calculation from any supporting document","Confirmation of whether the Standard Life SIPP still receives active employer contributions at the time of advice (the payment history in [P16] shows historical employer payments but does not confirm current activity)","Features or guarantees analysis for any of the five existing plans (confirmed absent from the report)"],"analysis":"The central and most 