t the reliance on it is weakened by the Standard Life charge error. The absence of any features-or-guarantees analysis for all five plans (confirmed absent by extraction metadata) is a separate concern under the highest-severity guidance. The Standard Life SIPP payment history [P16] shows employer contribution payments; the report does not address whether active employer contributions would be lost on transfer, which is a highest-severity trigger under the decision logic. The Scottish Widows encashment net receipt of £170 is presented as a benefit of the recommended action but is not corroborated by any passage in the pack. The vulnerability overlay: no vulnerability characteristics are identified in the evidence that would materially alter this finding beyond what is already noted. On balance, the Standard Life charge contradiction (narrative says saving, table says cost increase, and the table is computed on the wrong fund value) is a material concern that is not explained by any guard. The absence of features/guarantees analysis across all plans and the unaddressed employer contribution question for Standard Life compound the finding.","citations":[{"passageId":"P1","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"need to outperform your current plan by £4,672 in the first year (in monetary terms).","cells":null},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Based on a mid-growth rate of 2.94%, including our advice fees, the recommended plan would need to grow by 4.8% per year up to age 75 in order to meet the same value as your current plan.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Mid growth rate – (2.95%)","Existing pension £127,000","New solution £125,000","Reduction in yield 0.3% / 0.6%","Critical yield 3.2%","Effect of switching £2,000 or 1.6% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G"